{
 "videos": [
  {
   "id": "L0",
   "subject": "Why does your brain drop things?",
   "chapter": "Why does your brain drop things?",
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "You walk into the kitchen. You stop. Why are you here?",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2015/11/03/1261-144566598_medium.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "You had a reason. Three seconds ago it was right there. Now it's gone.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2015/08/10/267-135875613_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2023/02/07/149665-796691048_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "Later today you'll forget a name a few seconds after hearing it.",
     "clips": [
      "https://pixabay.com/get/ge0e7a99962d9565629880815f419e8be96ec51834048a7f5f74786a0f27d56fc6e06a9303c8b7a71cf354f582af560573474cc711a76e5f4334e10ae8f8a08f3_1280.jpg",
      "https://pixabay.com/get/g9fc51c159294060da538363cb934b8def14d0f8be4ee40c4193180735a17668ac626cd96e7ff10b07aca5f735b50ec643f05b37e9f944aa72c6724108cccaa62_1280.jpg"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "You'll reread something and lose most of it by tonight.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2019/09/25/27230-362518559_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2023/10/15/185096-874643413_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "And you'll remember parts of today that never happened the way you think.",
     "clips": [
      "https://pixabay.com/get/ge3df80a5bf09081ad59640f473448d2eebe89d648112e151f76a81686b911fc65b0d7b89708d620ca73c5231caa52d84fc9c17467d19b04f07be763f399deb5a_1280.jpg",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2020/06/01/40797-426939500_medium.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "So here's the question for this video. If your brain is so powerful, why does it keep dropping things?",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2021/01/06/61290-498228525_large.mp4",
      "https://pixabay.com/get/g7ec40b26894282f60b8cbe279706facccfc365c49226b3c3b19d8715f41d19e7c0239febdd06f65fd435c4fee17fb19df9cbfa25babbebc2ff32c9cf75bf77cb_1280.jpg"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "The answer is not what you'd expect. Your brain isn't failing. A lot of this forgetting is on purpose.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2024/12/28/249399_large.mp4",
      "https://pixabay.com/get/g2c8631626a306399154c891f9f300a2d215a3f181472d1496046647a469266ac619d91e6cbb8c4487fca87b69906c02acd2cf28e05bcb392144aa56a836123df_1280.jpg"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "Let's follow one ordinary day and catch your brain in the act.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2016/02/29/2337-157269912_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2021/07/08/80724-573496156_medium.mp4"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "settings": {
    "video_aspect": "16:9",
    "video_clip_duration": 4,
    "subtitle_position": "bottom",
    "font_size": 64,
    "bgm_type": "",
    "bgm_volume": 0.0,
    "voice_rate": 0.92,
    "subtitle_display_mode": "sentence",
    "subtitle_animation": "none"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "L1",
   "subject": "Morning: the doorway",
   "chapter": "Morning: the doorway",
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "It starts in the morning. You're in the bedroom, and you think: I need my keys.",
     "clips": [
      "https://pixabay.com/get/g954d0b1a40522f16fc7ea509bed6fccdbe11c6675e44516766e0e1dc05670e033b75dccdef1b0e12ef517337b7a1ecd41698ab0bff6b4cebf825e1145f909473_1280.jpg",
      "https://pixabay.com/get/g464d456657c56f15494ca9cdfadfe098ecb69604574ba63ee77532891f2fb3f76bc9e47975b32b9c35c273bbfd9faa81d3f1a30371392a1d4ad1daa1af6511a1_1280.jpg"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "You walk into the hallway, then the kitchen. And the thought is gone.",
     "clips": [
      "https://pixabay.com/get/ga48572ef1062477efa5b1633e8bd13565628924e9ce22389e972baacd2e3abdacc9ba238bb6bec06670a5ba8e4dec73d1520279c99c2b795e039ec43f61348ac_1280.jpg",
      "https://pixabay.com/get/g951e7df4b0bc59c42bad3812b7e8cd85556f586547248b7469bb1555ad36c6baf06289055e21e3b38cc30b7cc8c5676a7d7102977ad52ba104610ae759d97017_1280.jpg",
      "https://pixabay.com/get/g268c212e1f1a60e5042425aae57cb2d9bed5623510018aafa09742844883cd1ad367a9f8ccf679bd0496c93d9f6fbd170455da9fdd27f5cb8c3176027a208e51_1280.jpg"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "You've done this. Almost everyone has. And for years it looked like simple absent-mindedness.",
     "clips": [
      "https://pixabay.com/get/gef023c5596564818bdf084ba6e98150f7d9d34c9e7a8e4330daa83c71d79774392aa3dbb2838c2ec1ecf7b3ee64db5cbf33e395c15522ee08658f3da71071141_1280.jpg",
      "https://pixabay.com/get/g5876b374644928771c0e8d69b24afee139990f0a41e1858716605dd710d134b2b740c1648f314c00d06a0942375a8d255a3e8c2dbe30dbc8f6ae5952331e14b1_1280.jpg",
      "https://pixabay.com/get/g076a074334c6726c467268ee055c3eb3da1af4b4799fe255a76b06a91a09f70ec0adcd6508643ac34413ccbe25a5ce3a856b3746c0fdb1987aa88c82916d9285_1280.jpg"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "Then a team of memory researchers decided to test it.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2024/01/20/197489-905015030_medium.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2025/03/03/262187_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2024/01/20/197483-905015011_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "They set up rooms in a lab. People picked up an object at one table, carried it, and put it down at another table.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2024/06/11/216232_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2022/09/20/131988-751915297_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2016/08/11/4372-178617297_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "Sometimes the second table was in the same big room. Sometimes it was through a door, in a new room. The walking distance was the same.",
     "clips": [
      "repo:assets/2026-11-21-long/d01_doorway_setup.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "Then, right after they put it down, they were asked a simple question: what did you just put down?",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2022/08/28/129427-744370618_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "The people who had walked through a doorway forgot more. Same time. Same distance. The only difference was the door.",
     "clips": [
      "repo:assets/2026-11-21-long/d02_doorway_result.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "The researchers then tried something else. If the memory was left behind in the first room, maybe going back would bring it back.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2024/07/14/221151_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2017/06/20/10120-222371347_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "That's what we all do, right? Walk back to the bedroom and suddenly remember.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2020/07/02/43633-436237650_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2022/07/27/125777-735656057_large.mp4",
      "https://pixabay.com/get/gc2157a7cf415b911af8ce5f5ec9089f8c0ac55384055c1738cbef17566f1b07235f064c389f61677910874c1f132d6942df7811161fbe0ebf092ae20392a6ca9_1280.jpg"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "In a virtual version of the test, people who walked back into the first room did not do any better.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2022/08/27/129289-743827387_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2022/08/27/129288-743827388_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2022/08/27/129291-743827393_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "What mattered was how many new rooms they had entered. Each new room made the forgetting a little worse.",
     "clips": [
      "repo:assets/2026-11-21-long/d03_rooms.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "So what's going on? The best explanation is that your brain doesn't record your day as one long video.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2019/06/17/24499-344562763_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2015/10/16/992-142621119_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "It cuts it into scenes, a bit like a film editor.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2021/03/12/67825-523386992_medium.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2019/07/08/25058-347958039_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "A doorway is a strong signal that a scene has ended. When the scene ends, your brain files the old scene away and gets ready for the new one.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2016/08/22/4723-179738625_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2025/05/28/282141_medium.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2021/05/13/74016-550033213_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2018/11/13/19316-300877530_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "The plan you made in the bedroom gets filed with the bedroom.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2026/07/11/363408_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "That's not a bug. Think about how much you do in a day.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2015/12/18/1694-149530466_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "If every plan from every room stayed active, your head would be full of old, finished business.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2022/10/31/137186-765701394_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2024/09/21/232538_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "Closing the scene keeps your attention on where you are now. The cost is small: sometimes you lose a plan you still needed.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2025/07/23/293074_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2023/04/14/158961-818020185_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2021/02/23/66121-516532113_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "And this is the first clue to our question. Your brain isn't trying to keep everything. It's trying to keep what fits the moment you're in.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2017/04/15/8702-213454554_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2017/05/29/9395-219552658_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2017/08/29/11638-231758689_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "But the next part of your day is harder on memory, because now there are other people in the room.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2015/10/16/1057-142621433_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2015/10/16/1035-142621331_large.mp4"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "settings": {
