  {
   "id": "L3",
   "subject": "Lunch: why rereading fails",
   "chapter": "Lunch: why rereading fails",
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Lunch break. You reread some notes for later. A report, a lesson, a recipe. You feel like you've got it.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2016/02/29/2339-157269920_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2022/09/08/130649-747868380_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "More than a hundred years ago, one researcher tested his own memory in a strange way.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2022/02/08/107189-678129960_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2022/02/26/108947-683077032_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "He learned long lists of nonsense syllables, then measured how much work it took to learn them again after different delays.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2024/05/26/213746_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2022/02/09/107243-678130082_medium.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2020/04/22/36888-412873795_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "If relearning was fast, the memory was still partly there. If it was slow, it was mostly gone.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2019/09/01/26452-358778857_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2019/02/20/21549-318711490_medium.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2021/02/27/66519-517685720_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "In 2015, two researchers repeated this experiment. One of them, a young student, spent about seventy hours over seventy-five days learning and relearning lists.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2022/11/28/140805-776043754_medium.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2022/09/20/131989-751915301_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2022/11/28/140809-776043767_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "The pattern came back almost the same as before.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2023/02/01/148867-794951490_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2026/04/26/349089_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "Twenty minutes after learning a list, relearning it already took about half the original effort.",
     "clips": [
      "repo:assets/2026-11-21-long/d06_curve_a.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "After one day, about two-thirds. After a month, almost the whole effort again.",
     "clips": [
      "repo:assets/2026-11-21-long/d07_curve_b.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "This is the forgetting curve. Most of the loss happens early, in the first hours. Then it slows down.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2024/10/25/238231_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2021/02/27/66523-517685729_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2025/05/08/277521_medium.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "That sounds bad. But think about what it means. Your brain doesn't treat everything you see as worth keeping.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2015/10/16/1023-142621257_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2017/07/23/10831-226624994_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2023/09/13/180386-864121573_medium.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2020/10/31/53902-476396222_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "It waits for proof. Something you meet once and never use again fades fast.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2022/05/28/118418-714804804_medium.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2019/05/03/23263-334228232_medium.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "Something that keeps coming back gets kept.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2019/06/05/24216-340670744_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2016/07/26/4006-176282263_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "That's a smart rule for a brain that sees thousands of things a day. Most of them really don't matter.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2019/03/13/21985-323496013_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2018/11/29/19627-304735769_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "And here is where most of us get it wrong. We try to prove something matters by rereading it.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2022/11/26/140591-775389284_medium.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2022/11/26/140593-775389291_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "But rereading is a weak signal. The words look familiar, so it feels like you know them.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2025/04/23/273927_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2024/06/06/215472_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2021/08/05/84007-584870927_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "Your brain is mostly recognizing the page, not storing anything new.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2021/06/07/76775-560201196_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2021/08/05/84006-584870923_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "There's a much stronger signal, and we'll come back to it at the end, because it's the one thing in this video you can use today.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2020/07/11/44424-438662258_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2017/07/23/10822-226624975_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "First, let's get to the evening. Because the strangest part of memory isn't what you forget. It's what you remember that isn't true.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2022/09/13/131195-749706805_medium.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2024/09/07/230247_medium.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2024/09/14/231405_large.mp4"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "settings": {
    "video_aspect": "16:9",
    "video_clip_duration": 4,
    "subtitle_position": "bottom",
    "font_size": 64,
    "bgm_type": "",
    "bgm_volume": 0.0,
    "voice_rate": 0.92,
    "subtitle_display_mode": "sentence",
    "subtitle_animation": "none"
   },
   "title_card": "Lunch: why rereading fails"
  },
  {
   "id": "L4",
   "subject": "Evening: the day you remember",
   "chapter": "Evening: the day you remember",
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Evening. Someone asks you: how was your day?",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2023/07/21/172681-849651720_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "You don't replay the whole day. You can't. Instead you reach for a few moments. Usually the best or worst moment, and how it ended.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2015/08/09/180-135788610_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2022/07/07/123441-727986669_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "In one experiment, people put one hand in cold water, cold enough to hurt, for sixty seconds.",
     "clips": [
      "repo:assets/2026-11-21-long/d08_cold_a.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "In another trial, they did the same sixty seconds, then kept their hand in for thirty more seconds while the water got about one degree warmer.",
     "clips": [
      "repo:assets/2026-11-21-long/d09_cold_b.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "Still cold. Still uncomfortable. Just a little less so at the end.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2015/08/07/21-135711412_medium.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2023/03/14/154726-808562497_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "The second trial was longer. It was strictly more pain.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2021/08/04/83875-584870588_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2021/08/04/83874-584870586_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "But when they were asked which one they'd rather repeat, sixty-nine percent chose the longer one.",
     "clips": [
      "repo:assets/2026-11-21-long/d10_cold_result.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "Their memory didn't add up the pain minute by minute. It kept the worst moment and the end.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2018/11/06/19179-299997261_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2022/09/13/131160-749706756_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2016/09/05/4933-181466740_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "A better ending made the whole thing feel better, even though it lasted longer.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2022/12/05/141750-778319480_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2022/05/08/116388-707604365_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "So the day you describe tonight is already an edit. But it gets stranger.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2020/06/23/42967-434316750_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2023/10/08/184132-872701396_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "In a classic study, people watched short films of car crashes.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2025/05/01/275954_medium.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "Afterward they were asked one question: about how fast were the cars going when they hit each other?",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2020/04/05/35124-404782768_medium.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2016/01/11/1900-151662242_medium.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2016/07/28/4053-176749018_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "For other people, one word was changed. Instead of hit, the question said smashed.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2016/05/12/3145-166335957_large.mp4",
      "https://pixabay.com/get/g782a984eb2e1a8e8624cafecd1d154674c8f2601b04081b75da074fdf7f61e8e0a0dcddd1b5bb61c31c39f341eed3a160573a3471bb69fc1bef09f7c4fe5c336_1280.jpg",
      "https://pixabay.com/get/g9b3c13801a406a9eca36f86510388d3b2478e13331ec59545794e42568d91d73712d5cc326e4be24bd2bc8b1ae359b09d09e8bead283e0bb4db34101fab0054d_1280.jpg"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "That one word changed what people remembered.",
     "clips": [
      "https://pixabay.com/get/gd1d344452daa843634ddd1879189054fe1ce4d06b8ae9ca78150bbca3dcf4155e114983d8a761ee84521ca3a7729b29b4e124c10068284ec65ab801d56c1cdb5_1280.jpg"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "With smashed, the average guess was almost forty-one miles per hour. With hit, it was thirty-four. With contacted, under thirty-two.",
     "clips": [
      "repo:assets/2026-11-21-long/d11_speed.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "And a week later, everyone was asked: did you see any broken glass?",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2024/07/02/219140_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2021/02/01/63747-507811831_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2023/06/22/168313-838656609_medium.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "There was no broken glass in the film.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2024/06/28/218543_medium.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "Of the people who heard hit, fourteen percent said yes. Of the people who heard smashed, thirty-two percent said yes.",
     "clips": [
      "repo:assets/2026-11-21-long/d12_glass.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "Almost one in three remembered glass that never existed.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2021/11/05/94680-643084362_medium.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2023/11/15/189189-884961175_medium.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "This is what memory really is. Every time you remember something, you don't play back a recording. You rebuild it.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2020/09/15/50043-459915483_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2020/09/15/50044-459915485_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2024/01/28/198369-907598222_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "You use the bits you kept, plus what you know, plus what someone just said to you. Then you store it again, slightly changed.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2022/09/13/131159-749706751_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2020/04/18/36500-411342229_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2018/11/27/19598-303873067_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "So when you tell your friend about your day tonight, the story you tell becomes part of the memory.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2016/02/15/2174-155747455_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2024/12/09/245932_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "The words you choose, the parts you skip, the ending you give it.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2022/10/31/137251-766326171_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2022/10/31/137249-766326159_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "That sounds scary. But it's the same reason you can update what you know. A memory that can change is a memory that can learn.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2016/09/22/5635-183850259_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2017/02/24/7924-205529295_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2019/09/07/26637-360259342_medium.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "And that brings us to the night, and to the answer.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2023/05/24/164339-830461201_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2024/06/04/215246_large.mp4"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "settings": {
    "video_aspect": "16:9",
    "video_clip_duration": 4,
    "subtitle_position": "bottom",
    "font_size": 64,
    "bgm_type": "",
    "bgm_volume": 0.0,
    "voice_rate": 0.92,
    "subtitle_display_mode": "sentence",
    "subtitle_animation": "none"
   },
   "title_card": "Evening: the day you remember"
  },
  {
   "id": "L5",
   "subject": "Night: sleep, and the answer",
   "chapter": "Night: sleep, and the answer",
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "You go to bed. For you, the day is over. For your brain, there's still work to do.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2020/03/28/34473-402606909_large.mp4",
      "https://pixabay.com/get/g627fe347697c7bad9a71ac9300e295984edb2a5bc14a0c50ebfa08bbd771331331fd93005edc396bb399fc33b40d5464e3720d4e2c13bb4868b37f3b95b507de_1280.jpg",
      "https://pixabay.com/get/gc15753b9f4a54a317bc437f03a20a85f3b5d3cfbf27aad4b269913346e45b3679da31cf26f6de64c95dbbc66c362ce7dead22131ddc33041f11c76483347226c_1280.jpg"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "In one study, high-school students learned pairs of English and German words.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2023/04/15/159027-818026298_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2015/08/08/84-135733336_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "Some learned them in the evening, shortly before sleep. Others learned them in the morning and stayed awake all day.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2019/04/01/22518-328624723_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2017/08/06/11186-228573647_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2025/11/28/318540_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "The students who slept soon after learning forgot much less.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2023/08/19/176776-856056376_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "In a second test, students learned the words and then stayed awake all night.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2017/08/30/11722-231759069_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2017/01/07/7093-198553482_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "Even after a full night of recovery sleep, two days later they remembered less than students who had slept.",
     "clips": [
      "https://pixabay.com/get/g261bf72f0757eda9a44d817d7f8d715b6fa678f8475da80e8bf72d816624cd85d698fbba08ceada41ccdef4b5d3df0a5c681c367ef27f2447c0365aa0af63c55_1280.jpg",
      "https://pixabay.com/get/gc488a21fd15b2bf05f47b6a61797875d5afeb8a812b25dd34cff0dafd92b96e4ede03fceab36b06a7a38490492e08363_1280.jpg",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2022/11/28/140807-776043760_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "Sleep isn't just rest. It's when your brain sorts the day.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2020/06/23/42966-434316747_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2022/08/11/127621-739144659_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "Some of it gets kept and linked to what you already know. A lot of it gets let go.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2018/04/11/15522-264715974_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2017/08/16/11414-230268465_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2021/08/06/84086-584871133_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "So, the question we started with. If your brain is so powerful, why does it keep dropping things?",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2022/08/29/129472-744385475_medium.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2026/04/02/344025_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "Because dropping things is part of how it works.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2020/10/09/51961-467132005_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2023/12/10/192790-893446945_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "Brain scientists have argued that the goal of memory is not to keep a perfect record of the past.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2022/04/27/115324-704347593_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2015/11/11/1310-145386665_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "The goal is to help you make good decisions in a world that keeps changing.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2024/12/05/245028_medium.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2021/01/19/62368-504238671_medium.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "To do that, your brain actively weakens old connections. It lets go of details that don't repeat. It keeps the pattern and drops the noise.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2022/03/15/110879-689510470_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2018/12/17/20072-307163785_medium.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2021/10/06/91069-628462649_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "A brain that kept everything would get stuck in the past.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2018/04/16/15629-265015278_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "It would remember last year's password, last month's parking spot, the exact words of every conversation, and struggle to see what's new.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2020/07/25/45535-443134283_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2021/08/17/85428-588675541_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2025/09/24/306053_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "The doorway clears finished plans. The name fades because it had no meaning attached.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2022/09/10/130783-748347211_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2020/07/24/45471-443133954_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "The notes fade because nothing told your brain they mattered. Even the false glass is the price of a memory that can update.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2020/06/04/41121-427876237_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2017/04/25/8851-215589092_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2019/08/10/25922-353764102_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "So your brain forgets on purpose. But you can tell it what matters.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2019/04/05/22597-328624850_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "Here's the one thing to try today. Instead of rereading something, close it and test yourself. Try to write down or say what you remember.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2022/10/22/136081-764371377_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2022/07/08/123514-728292574_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2022/10/21/135877-764362178_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "In one experiment, students either read a short science text four times, or read it once and then tried to recall it three times.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2022/06/27/122231-724743698_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2023/04/15/159024-818026294_large.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2021/03/31/69628-531621074_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "Five minutes later, the rereaders did better.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2021/04/06/70216-533814725_medium.mp4",
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2024/06/06/215471_large.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "But one week later, the people who tested themselves remembered sixty-one percent. The rereaders remembered forty percent.",
     "clips": [
      "repo:assets/2026-11-21-long/d13_retrieval.mp4"
     ]
    },
    {
     "text": "And the rereaders were the ones who felt more confident.",
     "clips": [
      "https://cdn.pixabay.com/video/2022/09/04/130211-748134196_large.mp4"
     ]
